\begin_layout Standard
As we agreed,
 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{x}$
\end_inset

 runs from 
\begin_inset Formula $-M$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

.
 This means that the differences 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{x}^{\prime}-j_{x}$
\end_inset

 will run from 
\begin_inset Formula $-2M$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $2M$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 we need to use 
\begin_inset Formula $N=4M$
\end_inset

 points in FFT to obtain harmonics 
\begin_inset Formula $\pm2M$
\end_inset

.
 But it is more convenient to use
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
N=4M+1
\]

\end_inset

because when the number of points is odd,
 we have the both the 
\begin_inset Formula $-2M$
\end_inset

th and 
\begin_inset Formula $+2M$
\end_inset

th harmonics stored in the output array.
 E.g.
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $M=1$
\end_inset

,
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $N=4$
\end_inset

 we would have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccc}
{\tt V}_{0} & {\tt V}_{1} & {\tt V}_{2} & {\tt V}_{3}\\
V_{0} & V_{1} & V_{2} & V_{-1}
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

and then special care is needed to take the same 
\begin_inset Formula ${\tt V}_{2}$
\end_inset

 both for 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{-2}$
\end_inset

.
 Here 
\begin_inset Formula ${\tt V}_{i}$
\end_inset

 are the 0-based elements of the output of FFT.
 On the other hand,
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $N=4+1$
\end_inset

 we will conveniently have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{ccccc}
{\tt V}_{0} & {\tt V}_{1} & {\tt V}_{2} & {\tt V}_{3} & {\tt V}_{4}\\
V_{0} & V_{1} & V_{2} & V_{-2} & V_{-1}
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
[Note for Julia:
 After fftshift,
 a new,
 
\series bold
1-based array 
\series default
is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{ccccc}
\tilde{{\tt V}}_{1} & \tilde{{\tt V}}_{2} & \tilde{{\tt V}}_{3} & \tilde{{\tt V}}_{4} & \tilde{{\tt V}}_{5}\\
V_{-2} & V_{-1} & V_{0} & V_{1} & V_{2}
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

so that 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{i}=\tilde{{\tt V}}_{i+2M+1}$
\end_inset

.]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\begin_inset Formula ${\tt V}_{j'j}$
\end_inset

 matrix is then constructed as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\tt V}_{j'j}=\left(\begin{array}{ccc}
{\tt V}_{0} & {\tt V}_{4} & {\tt V}_{3}\\
{\tt V}_{1} & {\tt V}_{0} & {\tt V}_{4}\\
{\tt V}_{2} & {\tt V}_{1} & {\tt V}_{0}
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $V(x)$
\end_inset

 is real,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{m}=V_{-m}^{*}$
\end_inset

 so that this matrix will be Hermitian.
 If,
 additionally,
 
\begin_inset Formula $V(x)$
\end_inset

 is even,
 then the Fourier-components are real,
 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{m}=V_{-m}$
\end_inset

 and the matrix is real-symmetric.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For real 
\begin_inset Formula $V(x)$
\end_inset

,
 we can use rfft,
 which yields only positive harmonics.
 Again,
 we choose 
\begin_inset Formula $M=1$
\end_inset

 and compute FFT for 
\begin_inset Formula $N=4M+1$
\end_inset

,
 but this only yields the values 
\begin_inset Formula ${\tt V}_{0},{\tt V}_{1},{\tt V}_{2}$
\end_inset

 and we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{ccccc}
{\tt V}_{0} & {\tt V}_{1} & {\tt V}_{2} & {\tt V}_{2}^{*} & {\tt V}_{1}^{*}\\
V_{0} & V_{1} & V_{2} & V_{-2} & V_{-1}
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\tt V}_{j'j}=\left(\begin{array}{ccc}
{\tt V}_{0} & {\tt V}_{1}^{*} & {\tt V}_{2}^{*}\\
{\tt V}_{1} & {\tt V}_{0} & {\tt V}_{1}^{*}\\
{\tt V}_{2} & {\tt V}_{1} & {\tt V}_{0}
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Up to now we ignored normalisation factors;
 they are:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V_{m}=\frac{\Delta x}{L_{x}}{\tt V}_{m}=\frac{1}{N}{\tt V}_{m}\label{eq:V-normalisation}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

