\begin_layout Standard
Consider also converting a function 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

-space to 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-space,
 which must be performed for the initial condition in the dynamics.
 This looks simply as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\braket ju=u_{j}=\int_{0}^{L_{x}}\d x\:\braket jx\braket xu=\int_{0}^{L_{x}}\d x\:\frac{1}{\sqrt{L_{x}}}\e^{-\i\frac{2\pi jx}{L_{x}}}u(x).
\]

\end_inset

This amounts to taking FFT,
 but we also need to remember the normalisation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{j}=\frac{\Delta x}{\sqrt{L_{x}}}{\tt u}_{j}.
\]

\end_inset

This is not the same as when transforming operators,
 where we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\Delta x}{L_{x}}=\frac{1}{N}$
\end_inset

.
 But in fact it is easy to normalise vectors manually (using 
\family typewriter
normalize
\family default
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recall that the resulting 
\begin_inset Formula ${\tt u}_{j}$
\end_inset

 array will be laid in the order 
\begin_inset Formula $0,1,\ldots,M,-M,\ldots-1$
\end_inset

,
 but when constructing operator matrices we use the order from negative frequencies to positive,
 see table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:matrix-layout"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
 we need to perform fftshift (since these vectors must agree with the matrices that act on them).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 runs from 
\begin_inset Formula $-M$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

,
 we need 
\begin_inset Formula $N=2M+1$
\end_inset

 points.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Wannier states
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We want to diagonalise the position operator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}=\e^{\i\frac{2\pi}{L_{x}}\hat{x}}$
\end_inset

 constructed in the eigenbasis 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\phi_{j}}$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

.
 The required elements are
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X_{j'j}=\bra{\phi_{j'}}\e^{\i\frac{2\pi}{L_{x}}\hat{x}}\ket{\phi_{j}}.
\]

\end_inset

Our 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\phi_{j}}$
\end_inset

's are in momentum representation,
 so we need 
\begin_inset Formula $\e^{\i\frac{2\pi}{L_{x}}\hat{x}}$
\end_inset

 in momentum representation.
 Then we can simply construct 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}=\hat{S}^{\dagger}\e^{\i\frac{2\pi}{L_{x}}\hat{x}}\hat{S}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{S}$
\end_inset

 being the eigenvectors matrix.
 So we need
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\bra{\psi_{j'}}\e^{\i\frac{2\pi}{L_{x}}\hat{x}}\ket{\psi_{j}} & =\frac{1}{L_{x}}\intop_{0}^{L_{x}}\d x\,\e^{-\i\frac{2\pi j_{x}^{\prime}x}{L_{x}}}\e^{\i\frac{2\pi}{L_{x}}x}\e^{\i\frac{2\pi j_{x}x}{L_{x}}}=\frac{1}{L_{x}}\intop_{0}^{L_{x}}\d x\,\e^{\i\frac{2\pi}{L_{x}}(j-j'+1)}\\
 & =\delta_{j',j+1}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Then we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X_{ij}=(S^{\dagger})_{ik'}\delta_{k',k+1}S_{kj}=(S^{\dagger})_{i,k+1}S_{kj}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Diagonalising this 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}$
\end_inset

,
 we get the Wannier functions as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w_{a}(x)=\sum_{i}V_{i}^{(a)}\phi_{i}(x).
\]

\end_inset

In the code 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is discrete,
 and this is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w_{xa}=\sum_{i}V_{ia}\phi_{xi}\implies\hat{{\tt w}}=\hat{\psi}\hat{{\tt V}}.
\]

\end_inset

Mean energy of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{w_{a}}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{a}=\bra{w_{a}}\hat{h}\ket{w_{a}}=\sum_{m,m'}V_{m'}^{(a)*}V_{m}^{(a)}\bra{\phi_{m'}}\hat{h}\ket{\phi_{m}}=\sum_{m}|V_{m}^{(a)}|^{2}\varepsilon_{m}
\]

\end_inset

In the code,
 this is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{a}=\sum_{m}|V_{ma}|^{2}\varepsilon_{m}\implies\vec{{\tt E}}=\vec{\varepsilon}(|\hat{{\tt V}}|{\tt .^{2}})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This calculation uses the basis that assumes that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in[0,L_{x}]$
\end_inset

,
 so the corresponding eigenvalues have to be in the range 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,2\pi)$
\end_inset

 to prevent confusion.
 To this end we use 
\family typewriter
mod2pi
\family default
.
 However,
 in practice we have 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in[x_{0},x_{0}+L_{x}]$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
 we have to add 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{0}$
\end_inset

 to the eigenvalues.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
2D
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To diagonalise,
 we take the Fourier basis 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\psi_{j_{x}j_{y}}(\boldsymbol{r})=\frac{1}{\sqrt{L_{x}L_{y}}}\e^{\i\frac{2\pi(j_{x}x+j_{y}y)}{L_{x}L_{y}}},\qquad j_{x},j_{y}=-M,\ldots,0,\ldots,M
\]

\end_inset

The total number of values that 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{x},j_{y}$
\end_inset

 can attain (= the size of each block in 
\begin_inset Formula $\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}j_{y}^{\prime}}\hat{H}\ket{j_{x}j_{y}}$
\end_inset

) is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B=2M+1.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}j_{y}^{\prime}}-\i\partial_{x}\ket{j_{x}j_{y}}=\frac{1}{L^{2}}\iintop_{0}^{L}\e^{-\i\frac{2\pi(j_{x}^{\prime}x+j_{y}^{\prime}y)}{L}}\partial_{x}\e^{\i\frac{2\pi(j_{x}x+j_{y}y)}{L}}\d x\d y=-\i\frac{\i2\pi j_{x}}{L}=\frac{2\pi j_{x}}{L}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}j_{y}^{\prime}}\boldsymbol{q}\ket{j_{x}j_{y}} & =\boldsymbol{q}^{2}\delta_{j_{x}^{\prime},j_{x}}\delta_{j_{y}^{\prime},j_{y}}\nonumber \\
\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}j_{y}^{\prime}}-\i\delta\partial_{x}\ket{j_{x}j_{y}} & =\frac{2\pi\delta j_{x}}{L_{x}}\delta_{j_{x}^{\prime},j_{x}}\delta_{j_{y}^{\prime},j_{y}}\label{eq:diagonal}\\
\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}j_{y}^{\prime}}\delta^{2}\Delta\ket{j_{x}j_{y}} & =-\frac{(2\pi\delta)^{2}(j_{x}^{2}+j_{y}^{2})}{L_{x}L_{y}}\delta_{j_{x}^{\prime},j_{x}}\delta_{j_{y}^{\prime},j_{y}}\nonumber 
\end{align}

\end_inset

Next,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}j_{y}^{\prime}}H_{ab}(\boldsymbol{r})\ket{j_{x}j_{y}} & =\frac{1}{L^{2}}\iintop_{0}^{L}H_{ab}(\boldsymbol{r})\e^{\i\frac{2\pi(j_{x}-j_{x}^{\prime})x}{L_{x}}}\e^{\i\frac{2\pi(j_{y}-j_{y}^{\prime})y}{L_{y}}}\d x\d y\nonumber \\
 & =\frac{1}{L^{2}}\iintop_{0}^{L}H_{ab}(\boldsymbol{r})\e^{-\i\frac{2\pi(j_{x}^{\prime}-j_{x})x}{L_{x}}}\e^{-\i\frac{2\pi(j_{y}^{\prime}-j_{y})y}{L_{y}}}\d x\d y\nonumber \\
 & =\frac{1}{L^{2}}\tilde{H}_{ab}(j_{x}^{\prime}-j_{x},j_{y}^{\prime}-j_{y})\label{eq:H_ij-FT}
\end{align}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{H}_{ab}(m_{x},m_{y})$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $(m_{x},m_{y})$
\end_inset

th Fourier component of 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{ab}(\boldsymbol{r})$
\end_inset

 [see fft.lyx for refresh on the signs].
 As in the 1D case,
 the number of Harmonics is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
N=4M+1
\]

\end_inset

For 
\begin_inset Formula $M=1$
\end_inset

,
 the output of FFT and the harmonics they represent are 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
({\tt V}_{ij})=\begin{array}{ccccc}
{\tt V}_{00} & {\tt V}_{01} & {\tt V}_{02} & {\tt V}_{03} & {\tt V}_{04}\\
{\tt V}_{10} & {\tt V}_{11} & {\tt V}_{12} & {\tt V}_{13} & {\tt V}_{14}\\
{\tt V}_{20} & {\tt V}_{21} & {\tt V}_{22} & {\tt V}_{23} & {\tt V}_{24}\\
{\tt V}_{30} & {\tt V}_{31} & {\tt V}_{32} & {\tt V}_{33} & {\tt V}_{34}\\
{\tt V}_{40} & {\tt V}_{41} & {\tt V}_{42} & {\tt V}_{43} & {\tt V}_{44}
\end{array},\qquad(V_{m_{x}m_{y}})=\begin{array}{ccccc}
V_{00} & V_{01} & V_{02} & V_{0,-2} & V_{0,-1}\\
V_{10} & V_{11} & V_{12} & V_{1,-2} & V_{1,-1}\\
V_{20} & V_{21} & V_{22} & V_{2,-2} & V_{2,-1}\\
V_{-2,0} & V_{-2,1} & V_{-2,2} & V_{-2,-2} & V_{-2,-1}\\
V_{-1,0} & V_{-1,1} & V_{-1,2} & V_{-1,-2} & V_{-1,-1}
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

And now each row of 
\begin_inset Formula $({\tt V}_{ij})$
\end_inset

 turns to a block as was in 1D case,
 namely:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
({\tt H}_{ij})=\begin{array}{ccccccccc}
{\tt V}_{00} & {\tt V}_{04} & {\tt V}_{03} & |{\tt V}_{40} & {\tt V}_{44} & {\tt V}_{43} & |{\tt V}_{30} & {\tt V}_{34} & {\tt V}_{33}\\
{\tt V}_{01} & {\tt V}_{00} & {\tt V}_{04} & |{\tt V}_{41} & {\tt V}_{40} & {\tt V}_{44} & |{\tt V}_{31} & {\tt V}_{30} & {\tt V}_{34}\\
{\tt V}_{02} & {\tt V}_{01} & {\tt V}_{00} & |{\tt V}_{42} & {\tt V}_{41} & {\tt V}_{40} & |{\tt V}_{32} & {\tt V}_{31} & {\tt V}_{30}\\
{\tt V}_{10} & {\tt V}_{14} & {\tt V}_{13} & |{\tt V}_{00} & {\tt V}_{04} & {\tt V}_{03} & |{\tt V}_{40} & {\tt V}_{44} & {\tt V}_{43}\\
{\tt V}_{11} & {\tt V}_{10} & {\tt V}_{14} & |{\tt V}_{01} & {\tt V}_{00} & {\tt V}_{04} & |{\tt V}_{41} & {\tt V}_{40} & {\tt V}_{44}\\
{\tt V}_{12} & {\tt V}_{11} & {\tt V}_{10} & |{\tt V}_{02} & {\tt V}_{01} & {\tt V}_{00} & |{\tt V}_{42} & {\tt V}_{41} & {\tt V}_{40}\\
{\tt V}_{20} & {\tt V}_{24} & {\tt V}_{23} & |{\tt V}_{10} & {\tt V}_{14} & {\tt V}_{13} & |{\tt V}_{00} & {\tt V}_{04} & {\tt V}_{03}\\
{\tt V}_{21} & {\tt V}_{20} & {\tt V}_{24} & |{\tt V}_{11} & {\tt V}_{10} & {\tt V}_{14} & |{\tt V}_{01} & {\tt V}_{00} & {\tt V}_{04}\\
{\tt V}_{22} & {\tt V}_{21} & {\tt V}_{20} & |{\tt V}_{12} & {\tt V}_{11} & {\tt V}_{10} & |{\tt V}_{02} & {\tt V}_{01} & {\tt V}_{00}
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $V(x,y)$
\end_inset

 is real,
 then we can use RFT,
 which returns 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
({\tt V}_{ij})=\begin{array}{ccccc}
{\tt V}_{00} & {\tt V}_{01} & {\tt V}_{02} & {\tt V}_{03} & {\tt V}_{04}\\
{\tt V}_{10} & {\tt V}_{11} & {\tt V}_{12} & {\tt V}_{13} & {\tt V}_{14}\\
{\tt V}_{20} & {\tt V}_{21} & {\tt V}_{22} & {\tt V}_{23} & {\tt V}_{24}
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

This should be used to construct the lower triangle of 
\begin_inset Formula $({\tt H}_{ij})$
\end_inset

,
 with the upper following from conjugacy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}j_{y}^{\prime}}U_{D}(\boldsymbol{r})\ket{j_{x}j_{y}}$
\end_inset

 depends on the differences 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{x}\coloneqq j_{x}-j_{x}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{y}\coloneqq j_{y}-j_{y}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

,
 it becomes banded.
 Since it is 2D,
 it is in fact block-banded (with the bands indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{x}$
\end_inset

,
 say),
 with blocks being themselves banded (with these bands indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{y}$
\end_inset

,
 say).
 Therefore,
 when constructing 
\begin_inset Formula ${\tt H}$
\end_inset

,
 we fill the 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{y}$
\end_inset

th band of the 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{x}$
\end_inset

th block-band with 
\begin_inset Formula ${\tt U}_{m_{x},m_{y}}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula ${\tt U}$
\end_inset

 is the fft matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\boldsymbol{r})$
\end_inset

.
 Due to the symmetry 
\begin_inset Formula $x\leftrightarrow y$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula ${\tt U}_{m_{x},m_{y}}$
\end_inset

 is also symmetric (
\begin_inset Formula $m_{x}\leftrightarrow m_{y}$
\end_inset

).
 Therefore,
 when populating 
\begin_inset Formula ${\tt H}$
\end_inset

 with diagonal terms 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:diagonal"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 it does not matter which index we call 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{x}$
\end_inset

 and which 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{y}$
\end_inset

.
 Note,
 moreover,
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\boldsymbol{r})$
\end_inset

 is real and even (if the origin is adequately chosen),
 so that 
\begin_inset Formula ${\tt U}_{m_{x},m_{y}}$
\end_inset

 is also real and even.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Due to this dependence on the difference,
 we can use any shifted range for 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{x},j_{y}$
\end_inset

.
 That is,
 we can use 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{x},j_{y}=1,\ldots,2M+1$
\end_inset

 and apply 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:H_ij-FT"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 directly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider diagonalising repeatedly for different 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{q}$
\end_inset

,
 which is done in a dedicated 
\family typewriter
diagonalize!

\family default
 method:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}=0$
\end_inset

,
 we can make an efficient trick.
 The constructor of 
\family typewriter
DenseHamiltonian
\family default
 constructs 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\boldsymbol{q}}(\boldsymbol{r})$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{q}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
 in 
\family typewriter
diagonalize!

\family default
 we iterate over the passed 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{q}$
\end_inset

's.
 We only need to recalculate the diagonal of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\boldsymbol{q}}$
\end_inset

 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{q}$
\end_inset

.
 First,
 we need to extract the diagonal element of 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

.
 We do this by taking the diagonal element of the initial 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\boldsymbol{q}}$
\end_inset

 (which is initially constructed for 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{q}=0$
\end_inset

) for 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{x}=j_{y}=0$
\end_inset

 (in the code,
 this is the middle element of the diagonal of 
\family typewriter
H
\family default
).
 Then,
 we repeatedly (for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{q}$
\end_inset

) update the view of the diagonal of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\boldsymbol{q}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}\neq0$
\end_inset

,
 no such trick is possible because we have to recalculate the matrix square 
\begin_inset Formula $(-\i\delta\nabla+\boldsymbol{q}-\boldsymbol{A})^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that usually 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 is real,
 so we could use rfft,
 but it is much less convenient to handle (due to the output array being only half of the 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

usual
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

).
 Consider the actual timings:
 suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $M=100$
\end_inset

.
 This means that 
\begin_inset Formula ${\tt V}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $(2M+1)^{2}$
\end_inset

-by-
\begin_inset Formula $(2M+1)^{2}\approx$
\end_inset

 40
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

000-by-40
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

000 matrix [for Float64 it takes 
\begin_inset Formula $(2M+1)^{4}\times8/10^{9}\approx\unit[13]{GB}$
\end_inset

).
 Its diagonalisation,
 even with Arnoldi surely takes more than a minute (maybe tens of minutes).
 In this case we need to Fourier-transform an 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

-by-
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 matrix where 
\begin_inset Formula $N=4M+1$
\end_inset

.
 The timings are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
FFT:
 1.5
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

ms,
 5 MiB
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
RFFT:
 0.5
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

ms,
