\begin_layout Standard
The Laplace term is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}}\Delta\ket{j_{x}}=-\left(\frac{\pi j_{x}}{L_{x}}\right)^{2}\delta_{j_{x}^{\prime},j_{x}}
\]

\end_inset

Next,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}}V\ket{j_{x}} & =\frac{2}{L_{x}}\intop_{0}^{L}\sin\frac{\pi j_{x}^{\prime}x}{L_{x}}V(x)\sin\frac{\pi j_{x}x}{L_{x}}\d x\\
 & =\frac{1}{L_{x}}\intop_{0}^{L}V(x)\left(\cos\frac{\pi(j_{x}^{\prime}-j_{x})x}{L_{x}}-\cos\frac{\pi(j_{x}^{\prime}+j_{x})x}{L_{x}}\right)\d x\\
 & =\frac{1}{L_{x}}\intop_{0}^{L}V(x)\cos\frac{\pi(j_{x}^{\prime}-j_{x})x}{L_{x}}\d x\\
 & -\frac{1}{L_{x}}\intop_{0}^{L}V(x)\cos\frac{\pi(j_{x}^{\prime}+j_{x})x}{L_{x}}\d x
\end{align*}

\end_inset

The integral 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{L_{x}}\intop_{0}^{L}V(\boldsymbol{r})\cos\frac{\pi(j_{x}^{\prime}-j_{x})x}{L_{x}}\d x
\]

\end_inset

is the cos-transform term 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{m}$
\end_inset

 (times 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\d x}{2L_{x}}$
\end_inset

,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

 cancelling the one coming from FFTW) with 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{x}=j_{x}^{\prime}-j_{x}$
\end_inset

.
 
\series bold
The value of precisely this integral is stored in 
\family typewriter
buff
\family default
 (in the 
\family typewriter
target_rank=2
\family default
 case).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recall that 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{x}$
\end_inset

 runs from 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
 because of the term with sums 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{x}^{\prime}+j_{x}$
\end_inset

,
 we will need to calculate harmonics of 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 up to 
\begin_inset Formula $2M$
\end_inset

.
 But the transform will also include the zeroth harmonic (we don't have 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{x}=0$
\end_inset

 in the sin basis,
 but this is a 
\emph on
cosine
\emph default
 transform,
 for which the zeroth harmonic does make sense —
 like in the cis case,
 it is the average of the function),
 so the number of harmonics to calculate (and the number of points to use for discretising space) is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
N=2M+1.
\]

\end_inset

(It is recommended to take transform with the number of points 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{n}-1$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

,
 leading to 
\begin_inset Formula $2M+1=2^{n}-1\implies M=2^{n-1}-1$
\end_inset

.
 So 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 given by power of two minus one is recommend,
 but this is not that important because we do not use so many points for the speed of FFT to matter).
\end_layout

