\begin_layout Standard
Consider also converting a function 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

-space to 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-space,
 which must be performed for the initial condition in the dynamics.
 This looks simply as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\braket ju=u_{j}=\int_{0}^{L_{x}}\d x\:\braket jx\braket xu=\int_{0}^{L_{x}}\d x\:\sqrt{\frac{2}{\zeta_{j_{x}}L_{x}}}\cos\frac{\pi j_{x}x}{L_{x}}u(x).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The integral 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{\frac{2}{L_{x}}}\intop_{0}^{L}V(\boldsymbol{r})\cos\frac{\pi j_{x}x}{L_{x}}\d x
\]

\end_inset

is the cos-transform term 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{j_{x}}$
\end_inset

 (times 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\d x}{\sqrt{2L_{x}}}$
\end_inset

;
 FFTW yields a factor of 2,
 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $2/\sqrt{2}$
\end_inset

 yields the required 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{2}$
\end_inset

 in the above integral).
 
\series bold
The value of precisely this integral is stored in 
\family typewriter
buff
\family default
 (in the 
\family typewriter
target_rank=1
\family default
 case).
 
\series default
As in the 2D case,
 we do not include the factor of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{1/\zeta_{j_{x}}}$
\end_inset

 here (although in this 1D case we could),
 but take it into account when constructing the vector in 
\family typewriter
fft_to_vector!
\family default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the transform we need 
\begin_inset Formula $M+1$
\end_inset

 points.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To get transform back,
 we write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(x)=\sqrt{\frac{2}{\zeta_{j_{x}}L_{x}}}\sum_{j=0}^{M}\cos\frac{\pi jx}{L_{x}}u_{j}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{L_{x}}}u_{j}+\sqrt{\frac{2}{L_{x}}}\sum_{j=1}^{M}\cos\frac{\pi jx}{L_{x}}u_{j}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
When is 
\family typewriter
H
\family default
 real?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In all cases,
 for 
\family typewriter
H
\family default
 to be real,
 the basic necessary condition is that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 be real.
 In particular,
 this means that there is no 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}$
\end_inset

 and no 
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma$
\end_inset

.
 The diagonal potentials 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{ii}(\boldsymbol{r})$
\end_inset

 are basically always real (unless you consider some exotic non-hermitian case),
 but the off-diagonal couplings 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{ij}(\boldsymbol{r})$
\end_inset

 may be complex in a large number of problems.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{ii}(\boldsymbol{r})$
\end_inset

 are real,
 we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
1D:
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{ii}(\boldsymbol{r})$
\end_inset

 are even,
 then Fourier transform is real (and even),
 so the constructed 
\family typewriter
H
\family default
 is real.
 However,
 the eigenfunctions might be complex.
 In fact,
 they can be chosen real,
 and in fact the numerical calculation yields functions that are either purely real or purely imaginary.
 But in case of degeneracies,
 the code yields complex functions.
 So the eigenfunctions have to be stored as complex.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
2D:
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Periodic case:
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{ii}(\boldsymbol{r})$
\end_inset

 are even,
 then 
\family typewriter
H
\family default
 is real.
 But eigenfunctions are stored as complex,
 as in the 1D case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Non-periodic case:
 
\family typewriter
H
\family default
 is real (because cos-transform of 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 is always real).
 The eigenfunctions are real (since 
\family typewriter
H
\family default
 is real,
 
\family typewriter
eigen
\family default
 returns a real eigenfunction matrix 
\family typewriter
V
\family default
,
 and the basis is also real)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection
Examples
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
2D Lambda
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We consider
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}=\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}^{2}+\hat{V}(\boldsymbol{r}),\qquad\hat{V}(\boldsymbol{r})=(-\Delta-\i\frac{\Gamma}{2})\ketbra ee+\sum_{j=1}^{2}\left(\Omega_{j}(\boldsymbol{r})\ketbra ej+{\rm H.c.}\right)
\]

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}=\begin{pmatrix}\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}^{2} & 0 & \Omega_{1}^{*}(\boldsymbol{r})\\
0 & \hat{\boldsymbol{p}}^{2} & \Omega_{2}^{*}(\boldsymbol{r})\\
\Omega_{1}(\boldsymbol{r}) & \Omega_{2}(\boldsymbol{r}) & \hat{\boldsymbol{p}}^{2}-\Delta-\i\frac{\Gamma}{2}
\end{pmatrix}
\]

\end_inset

with (we do some renaming compared to the paper)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\Omega_{1}(\boldsymbol{r}) & =\frac{1}{2}\Omega_{10}\\
\Omega_{2}(\boldsymbol{r}) & =\frac{1}{2}[-\Omega_{-}\e^{\i\frac{\chi}{2}}\cos(x-y)+\Omega_{+}\e^{-\i\frac{\chi}{2}}\cos(x+y)]
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Using
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\epsilon=\frac{\Omega_{10}}{\sqrt{\Omega_{+}^{2}+\Omega_{-}^{2}}},\qquad\epsilon_{{\rm c}}=\frac{\Omega_{-}}{\Omega_{+}}
\]

\end_inset

we express 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{-}$
\end_inset

 in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{{\rm p}}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\epsilon=\frac{\Omega_{10}}{\Omega_{+}\sqrt{1+\epsilon_{{\rm c}}^{2}}}\implies\Omega_{+}=\frac{\Omega_{10}}{\epsilon\sqrt{1+\epsilon_{{\rm c}}^{2}}},\quad\Omega_{-}=\frac{\epsilon_{{\rm c}}\Omega_{10}}{\epsilon\sqrt{1+\epsilon_{{\rm c}}^{2}}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Periodicity of the Hamiltonian due to 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{2}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $L\coloneqq2\pi$
\end_inset

.
 Next,
 we transform 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\boldsymbol{q}}(\boldsymbol{r})\to\e^{\i\boldsymbol{q}\boldsymbol{r}}\hat{H}(\boldsymbol{r})$
\end_inset

,
 where the BZ may be chosen as 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{x}\in[0;\frac{2\pi}{L}]=[0;1]$
\end_inset

.
 We take the Fourier basis 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\psi_{n_{x}n_{y}}(\boldsymbol{r})=\frac{1}{\sqrt{L}}\e^{\i\frac{2\pi(j_{x}x+j_{y}y)}{L}}
\]

\end_inset

Using 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{2}(\boldsymbol{r})=\frac{1}{2}[-\Omega_{-}\e^{\i\frac{\chi}{2}}\frac{1}{2}(\e^{\i x}\e^{-\i y}+\e^{-\i x}\e^{\i y})+\Omega_{+}\e^{-\i\frac{\chi}{2}}\frac{1}{2}(\e^{\i x}\e^{\i y}+\e^{-\i x}\e^{-\i y})$
\end_inset

,
 we easily write down the Fourier images:
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Including a shift 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}j_{y}^{\prime}}\cos(x+\delta_{x}-(y+\delta_{y}))\ket{j_{x}j_{y}} & =\frac{1}{(2\pi)^{2}}\iintop_{0}^{2\pi}\frac{\e^{\i\delta_{x}}\e^{-\i\delta_{y}}\e^{\i(x-y)}+\e^{-\i\delta_{x}}\e^{\i\delta_{y}}\e^{-\i(x-y)}}{2}\e^{\i((j_{x}-j_{x}^{\prime})x+(j_{y}-j_{y}^{\prime})y)}\d x\d y\\
 & =\frac{1}{(2\pi)^{2}}\iintop_{0}^{2\pi}\frac{\e^{\i\delta_{x}}\e^{-\i\delta_{y}}\e^{\i((j_{x}-j_{x}^{\prime}+1)x+(j_{y}-j_{y}^{\prime}-1)y)}+\e^{-\i\delta_{x}}\e^{\i\delta_{y}}\e^{\i((j_{x}-j_{x}^{\prime}-1)x+(j_{y}-j_{y}^{\prime}+1)y)}}{2}\d x\d y\\
 & =\frac{\e^{\i\delta_{x}}\e^{-\i\delta_{y}}\delta_{j_{x}-j_{x}^{\prime}+1,0}\delta_{j_{y}-j_{y}^{\prime}-1,0}+\e^{-\i\delta_{x}}\e^{\i\delta_{y}}\delta_{j_{x}-j_{x}^{\prime}-1,0}\delta_{j_{y}-j_{y}^{\prime}+1,0}}{2}\\
 & =\frac{\e^{\i\delta_{x}}\e^{-\i\delta_{y}}\delta_{j_{x}+1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}-1,j_{y}^{\prime}}+\e^{-\i\delta_{x}}\e^{\i\delta_{y}}\delta_{j_{x}-1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}+1,j_{y}^{\prime}}}{2}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}j_{y}^{\prime}}\cos(x+\delta_{x}+y+\delta_{y})\ket{j_{x}j_{y}} & =\frac{1}{(2\pi)^{2}}\iintop_{0}^{2\pi}\frac{\e^{\i\delta_{x}}\e^{\i\delta_{y}}\e^{\i(x+y)}+\e^{-\i\delta_{x}}\e^{-\i\delta_{y}}\e^{-\i(x+y)}}{2}\e^{\i((j_{x}-j_{x}^{\prime})x+(j_{y}-j_{y}^{\prime})y)}\d x\d y\\
 & =\frac{1}{(2\pi)^{2}}\iintop_{0}^{2\pi}\frac{\e^{\i\delta_{x}}\e^{\i\delta_{y}}\e^{\i((j_{x}-j_{x}^{\prime}+1)x+(j_{y}-j_{y}^{\prime}+1)y)}+\e^{-\i\delta_{x}}\e^{-\i\delta_{y}}\e^{\i((j_{x}-j_{x}^{\prime}-1)x+(j_{y}-j_{y}^{\prime}-1)y)}}{2}\d x\d y\\
 & =\frac{\e^{\i\delta_{x}}\e^{\i\delta_{y}}\delta_{j_{x}-j_{x}^{\prime}+1,0}\delta_{j_{y}-j_{y}^{\prime}+1,0}+\e^{-\i\delta_{x}}\e^{-\i\delta_{y}}\delta_{j_{x}-j_{x}^{\prime}-1,0}\delta_{j_{y}-j_{y}^{\prime}-1,0}}{2}\\
 & =\frac{\e^{\i\delta_{x}}\e^{\i\delta_{y}}\delta_{j_{x}+1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}+1,j_{y}^{\prime}}+\e^{-\i\delta_{x}}\e^{-\i\delta_{y}}\delta_{j_{x}-1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}-1,j_{y}^{\prime}}}{2}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}j_{y}^{\prime}}\Omega_{1}(\boldsymbol{r})\ket{j_{x}j_{y}} & =\frac{1}{2}\Omega_{{\rm p}}\delta_{j_{x}^{\prime},j_{x}}\delta_{j_{y}^{\prime},j_{y}}\\
\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}j_{y}^{\prime}}\Omega_{2}(\boldsymbol{r})\ket{j_{x}j_{y}} & =\frac{\Omega_{{\rm p}}}{2\epsilon\sqrt{1+\epsilon_{{\rm c}}^{2}}}\left[-\e^{\i\frac{\chi}{2}}(\e^{\i\delta_{x}}\e^{-\i\delta_{y}}\delta_{j_{x}+1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}-1,j_{y}^{\prime}}+\e^{-\i\delta_{x}}\e^{\i\delta_{y}}\delta_{j_{x}-1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}+1,j_{y}^{\prime}})+\epsilon_{{\rm c}}\e^{-\i\frac{\chi}{2}}(\e^{\i\delta_{x}}\e^{\i\delta_{y}}\delta_{j_{x}+1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}+1,j_{y}^{\prime}}+\e^{-\i\delta_{x}}\e^{-\i\delta_{y}}\delta_{j_{x}-1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}-1,j_{y}^{\prime}})\right]
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}j_{y}^{\prime}}\Omega_{1}(\boldsymbol{r})\ket{j_{x}j_{y}} & =\frac{1}{2}\Omega_{10}\delta_{j_{x}^{\prime},j_{x}}\delta_{j_{y}^{\prime},j_{y}}\\
\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}j_{y}^{\prime}}\Omega_{2}(\boldsymbol{r})\ket{j_{x}j_{y}} & =\frac{\Omega_{+}}{4}\left[-\e^{\i\frac{\chi}{2}}(\delta_{j_{x}+1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}-1,j_{y}^{\prime}}+\delta_{j_{x}-1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}+1,j_{y}^{\prime}})+\epsilon_{{\rm c}}\e^{-\i\frac{\chi}{2}}(\delta_{j_{x}+1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}+1,j_{y}^{\prime}}+\delta_{j_{x}-1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}-1,j_{y}^{\prime}})\right]
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
2D Lambda Topological
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ignoring 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\Omega_{1}(\boldsymbol{r}) & =\Omega_{0}(\sin(x+y)-\i\sin(x-y)]\\
\Omega_{2}(\boldsymbol{r}) & =\epsilon\Omega_{0}\left[1+\frac{\nu}{2}\cos(2x)+\frac{\nu}{2}\cos(2y)\right]
\end{align*}

\end_inset

The common period is 
\begin_inset Formula $L\coloneqq2\pi$
\end_inset

.
 So we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}j_{y}^{\prime}}\Omega_{1}(\boldsymbol{r})\ket{j_{x}j_{y}} & =\frac{\Omega_{0}}{2}\left[-\i(\delta_{j_{x}+1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}+1,j_{y}^{\prime}}-\delta_{j_{x}-1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}-1,j_{y}^{\prime}})-(\delta_{j_{x}+1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}-1,j_{y}^{\prime}}-\delta_{j_{x}-1,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}+1,j_{y}^{\prime}})\right]\\
\bra{j_{x}^{\prime}j_{y}^{\prime}}\Omega_{2}(\boldsymbol{r})\ket{j_{x}j_{y}} & =\epsilon\Omega_{0}\left[1+\frac{\nu}{4}(\delta_{j_{x}+2,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y},j_{y}^{\prime}}+\delta_{j_{x}-2,j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y},j_{y}^{\prime}})+\frac{\nu}{4}(\delta_{j_{x},j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y},j_{y}^{\prime}+2}+\delta_{j_{x},j_{x}^{\prime}}\delta_{j_{y}-2,j_{y}^{\prime}})\right]
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Evolution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now consider the evolution
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\i\psi'=\hat{H}\psi+g|\psi|^{2}\psi
\]

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\psi'=-\i\hat{H}\psi-\i g|\psi|^{2}\psi
\]

\end_inset

 which can also be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\psi'=\hat{A}(\psi)\psi,\qquad\hat{A}(\psi)\coloneqq-\i\hat{H}-\i g|\psi|^{2}
\]

\end_inset

When we switch to 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-space,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 becomes a matrix,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 becomes a vector (with elements showing Fourier coefficients).
 Then,
 
\begin_inset Formula $|\psi|^{2}$
\end_inset

 means element-wise abs2,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g|\psi|^{2}\psi$
\end_inset

 also means element-wise product between 
\begin_inset Formula $g|\psi|^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

.
 Clearly,
 this product can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula $g\hat{\Psi}\psi$
\end_inset

,
 where the diagonal of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Psi}$
\end_inset

 contains 
\begin_inset Formula $|\psi|^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{A}$
\end_inset

 should be constructed in momentum space as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{A}=-\i\hat{H}-\i g\hat{I}|\psi|^{2}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In multi-component case,
 we can have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\i\psi_{1}^{\prime} & =(\hat{H}_{11}+g_{11}|\psi_{1}|^{2}+g_{12}|\psi_{2}|^{2})\psi_{1}+\hat{H}_{12}\psi_{2}\\
\i\psi_{2}^{\prime} & =(\hat{H}_{22}+g_{21}|\psi_{1}|^{2}+g_{22}|\psi_{2}|^{2})\psi_{2}+\hat{H}_{21}\psi_{2}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{ij}$
\end_inset

 are the blocks of the components.
 So here we have to separately update the diagonals of each diagonal block (
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{11}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{22}$
\end_inset

).
 Again we explicitly show the signs:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\psi_{1}^{\prime} & =(-\i\hat{H}_{11}-\i g_{11}|\psi_{1}|^{2}-\i g_{12}|\psi_{2}|^{2})\psi_{1}-\i\hat{H}_{12}\psi_{2}\\
\psi_{2}^{\prime} & =(-\i\hat{H}_{22}-\i g_{21}|\psi_{1}|^{2}-\i g_{22}|\psi_{2}|^{2})\psi_{2}-\i\hat{H}_{21}\psi_{2}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When we do imaginary time,
 we replace 
\begin_inset Formula $\i\partial_{t}\to-\partial_{t}$
\end_inset

 and the equation becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\psi'=-\hat{H}\psi-g|\psi|^{2}\psi.
\]

\end_inset

Note the minuses.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{A}(\psi)\coloneqq-\hat{H}-g|\psi|^{2}$
\end_inset

 and before solving starts we save the diagonal of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{A}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $g=0$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.
 the diagonal of 
\begin_inset Formula $-\hat{H}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Chemical potential
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To get chemical potential,
 we need to integrate 
\begin_inset Formula $\int|\psi(x)|^{4}\d x$
\end_inset

.
 We must be careful with normalisation factors.
 First,
 we prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $\int\psi^{*}(x)\psi(x)\d x$
\end_inset

 is just the dot product of 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-space vectors:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int\psi^{*}(x)\psi(x)\d x=\int\braket{\psi}x\braket x{\psi}\d x=\sum_{p'p}\int\braket{\psi}{p'}\braket{p'}x\braket xp\braket p{\psi}\d x=\sum_{p'p}\int\braket{\psi}{p'}\frac{1}{\sqrt{L}}\e^{-\i p'x}\frac{1}{\sqrt{L}}\e^{\i px}\braket p{\psi}\d x=\sum_{p}\int\braket{\psi}p\frac{1}{L}\braket p{\psi}\d x=\sum_{p}\braket{\psi}p\braket p{\psi}
\]

\end_inset

Now consider the integrand squared:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\int[\psi^{*}(x)\psi(x)]^{2}\d x & =\int\left(\sum_{p'p}\braket{\psi}{p'}\frac{1}{\sqrt{L}}\e^{-\i p'x}\frac{1}{\sqrt{L}}\e^{\i px}\braket p{\psi}\right)^{2}\d x\\
 & =\sum_{p'p}\sum_{k'k}\frac{1}{L^{2}}\int\braket{\psi}{p'}\braket p{\psi}\braket{\psi}{k'}\braket k{\psi}\e^{\i(p-p'+k-k')x}\d x\\
 & =\frac{1}{L}\sum_{p'pk}\braket{\psi}{p'}\braket p{\psi}\braket{\psi}{k+p-p'}\braket k{\psi}\\
 & =\frac{1}{L}\sum_{p'pk}\psi_{p'}^{*}\psi_{p}\psi_{k+p-p'}^{*}\psi_{k}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Nonlinear term in 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-space
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Next we must figure out how the nonlinear term looks in momentum space.
 In coordinate space we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\i\psi'(x)=\hat{H}(x)\psi(x)+g|\psi(x)|^{2}\psi(x)
\]

\end_inset

We can write this in more suggestive form assuming that coordinate is discrete:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\i\psi_{x}^{\prime}=\sum_{x'}H_{xx'}\psi_{x'}+g|\psi_{x}|^{2}\psi_{x}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{x'}H_{xx'}\psi_{x'}=\sum_{x'}H_{xx'}\delta_{x'x'}\psi_{x'}=\sum_{x'}H_{xx'}(U^{\dagger})_{x'p}U_{px'}\psi_{x'}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

Then we can introduce wf in momentum space according to 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{p}=(\hat{U}^{\dagger})_{px}\psi_{x}$
\end_inset

.
 Specifically,
 we write (using 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{I}=\hat{U}^{\dagger}\hat{U}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{ij}=(\hat{U}^{\dagger})_{ik}(\hat{U})_{kj}$
\end_inset

) 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\i\sum_{x}(\hat{U}^{\dagger})_{px}\psi'_{x}=\sum_{x,x',p'}(\hat{U}^{\dagger})_{px}H_{xx'}U_{x'p'}(\hat{U}^{\dagger})_{p'x'}\psi_{x'}+g\sum_{x}(\hat{U}^{\dagger})_{px}|\psi_{x}|^{2}\psi_{x}
\]

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\i\psi'_{p}=\sum_{p'}\hat{H}_{pp'}\psi_{p'}+g\sum_{x}(\hat{U}^{\dagger})_{px}|\psi_{x}|^{2}\psi_{x}.
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The nonlinear term is not obvious.
 So we write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{x}U_{px}|\psi_{x}|^{2}\psi_{x}=\sum_{x}U_{px}|\psi_{x}|^{2}(\hat{U}^{\dagger})_{xp'}U_{p'x}\psi_{x}
\]

\end_inset

Let us use the inverse transformation 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{U}^{\dagger})_{xp}\psi_{p}=\psi_{x}$
\end_inset

 to write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{x}U_{px}|\psi_{x}|^{2}\psi_{x}=\sum_{x,p}U_{px}|(\hat{U}^{\dagger})_{xp}\psi_{p}|^{2}(\hat{U}^{\dagger})_{xp}\psi_{p}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

To treat 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{x}(\hat{U}^{\dagger})_{px}|\psi_{x}|^{2}\psi_{x}$
\end_inset

 we write down everything we now:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\psi_{x}=\sum_{p}\frac{\e^{\i2\pi p\frac{x}{L}}}{\sqrt{L}}\psi_{p}\Longleftrightarrow\psi_{x}=\sum_{p}U_{xp}\psi_{p}\Longleftrightarrow\braket x{\psi}=\sum_{p}\braket xp\braket p{\psi}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\psi_{p}=\sum_{x}\frac{\e^{-\i2\pi p\frac{x}{L}}}{\sqrt{L}}\psi_{x}\Longleftrightarrow\psi_{p}=\sum_{x}(U_{xp})^{*}\psi_{x}=\sum_{x}(\hat{U}^{\dagger})_{px}\psi_{x}\Longleftrightarrow\braket p{\psi}=\sum_{x}\braket px\braket x{\psi}.
\]

\end_inset

Here we assumed that the upper expression 
\emph on
defines
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{xp}\coloneqq\frac{\e^{\i2\pi p\frac{x}{L}}}{\sqrt{L}}$
\end_inset

,
 and then we can use 
\begin_inset Formula $(U_{xp})^{*}$
\end_inset

 in the lower expression.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Anyway,
 we now write (this is in fact obvious because we are just taking Fourier transform of the equation;
 one can say that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{x}(\hat{U}^{\dagger})_{px}$
\end_inset

 is the Fourier-transform operator 
\begin_inset Formula $\intop\d x\,\frac{\e^{-\i2\pi p\frac{x}{L}}}{\sqrt{L}}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{x}(\hat{U}^{\dagger})_{px}|\psi_{x}|^{2}\psi_{x} & =\intop\d x\,\frac{\e^{-\i2\pi p\frac{x}{L}}}{\sqrt{L}}|\psi(x)|^{2}\psi(x)=\intop\d x\,\frac{\e^{-\i2\pi p\frac{x}{L}}}{\sqrt{L}}|\braket x{\psi}|^{2}\braket x{\psi}=\intop\d x\,\frac{\e^{-\i2\pi p\frac{x}{L}}}{\sqrt{L}}|\sum_{k'}\braket x{k'}\braket{k'}{\psi}|^{2}\sum_{k}\braket xk\braket k{\psi}\\
 & =\intop\d x\,\frac{\e^{-\i2\pi p\frac{x}{L}}}{\sqrt{L}}|\sum_{k'}\frac{\e^{\i2\pi k'\frac{x}{L}}}{\sqrt{L}}\braket{k'}{\psi}|^{2}\sum_{k}\frac{\e^{\i2\pi k\frac{x}{L}}}{\sqrt{L}}\braket k{\psi}\\
 & =\frac{1}{L^{2}}\sum_{k}\intop\d x\,\e^{\i2\pi(k-p)\frac{x}{L}}\braket k{\psi}|\sum_{k'}\e^{\i2\pi k'\frac{x}{L}}\braket{k'}{\psi}|^{2}\\
 & =\frac{1}{L^{2}}\sum_{k}\intop\d x\,\e^{\i2\pi(k-p)\frac{x}{L}}\braket k{\psi}\sum_{k'k''}\e^{\i2\pi k'\frac{x}{L}}\braket{k'}{\psi}\e^{-\i2\pi k''\frac{x}{L}}\braket{\psi}{k''}\\
 & =\frac{1}{L^{2}}\sum_{k,k',k''}\intop\d x\,\e^{\i2\pi(k-p+k'-k'')\frac{x}{L}}\braket k{\psi}\braket{k'}{\psi}\braket{\psi}{k''}\\
 & =\frac{1}{L}\sum_{k,k'}\braket k{\psi}\braket{k'}{\psi}\braket{\psi}{k-p+k'}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

The equation is thus
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\i\psi_{p}^{\prime}=\sum_{p'}\hat{H}_{pp'}\psi_{p'}+\frac{g}{L}\sum_{k,k'}\psi_{k}\psi_{k'}\psi_{k+k'-p}^{*}.
\]

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\psi_{p}^{\prime}=-\i\sum_{p'}\hat{H}_{pp'}\psi_{p'}-\i\frac{g}{L}\sum_{k,k'}\psi_{k}\psi_{k'}\psi_{k+k'-p}^{*}.
\]

\end_inset

Strictly speaking,
 the indices 
\begin_inset Formula $k,k',p$
\end_inset

 run from 
\begin_inset Formula $-M$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

,
 but there is no difference if we assume they run from 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $B=2M+1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the code,
 we use
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{k,k'}\psi_{k}\psi_{k'}\psi_{k'+k-p}^{*}=\sum_{k=1}^{B}\psi_{k}\sum_{k'=1}^{B}\psi_{k'}\psi_{k'+k-p}^{*}
\]

\end_inset

Bounds:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
From 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{k}\psi_{k'}\psi_{k'+k-p}^{*}$
\end_inset

 we see that 
\begin_inset Formula $k+k'-p$
\end_inset

 must be at least 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 for a given 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 index 
\begin_inset Formula $k'$
\end_inset

 must be at least 
\begin_inset Formula $p+1-k$
\end_inset

.
 But also 
\begin_inset Formula $k'$
\end_inset

 must be at least 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Thee index 
\begin_inset Formula $k+k'-p$
\end_inset

 must be at most 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 for a given 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 index 
\begin_inset Formula $k'$
\end_inset

 must be at most 
\begin_inset Formula $p+B-k$
\end_inset

.
 But also 
\begin_inset Formula $k'$
\end_inset

 must be at most 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So we actually have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{k=1}^{B}\psi_{k}\sum_{k'=\max(1,1+p-k)}^{\min(B,B+p-k)}\psi_{k'}\psi_{k'+k-p}^{*}\label{eq:p-space-nln}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

